1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,280
Listen to a talk in an economics class.

2
00:00:02,280 --> 00:00:06,860
Imagine that you have a full time job

3
00:00:07,060 --> 00:00:09,170
but you want to take a year off to go back to school.

4
00:00:09,170 --> 00:00:11,300
What is it going to cost you

5
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:15,210
Of course there's the cost of tuition for your classes, plus living expenses.

6
00:00:15,210 --> 00:00:18,180
But what about the money you're no longer earning at your job

7
00:00:18,180 --> 00:00:20,229
because you're now a student?

8
00:00:20,229 --> 00:00:22,000
We call that the opportunity cost.

9
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,280
Opportunity cost refers to the value of the next best alternative that must be given up when making a choice.

10
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:31,140
It's a fundamental principle in economics that helps individuals

11
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:35,000
and businesses understand the true cost of their decisions.

12
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,308
My exercise in imagination illustrates a personal decision,

13
00:00:38,308 --> 00:00:42,030
but businesses also face opportunity costs in their operations.

14
00:00:42,030 --> 00:00:48,140
For instance, a company may choose to invest in new equipment rather than spend money to expand its product line.

15
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:54,040
The opportunity cost of this decision is the potential revenue that could have been generated from the new products.

16
00:00:54,040 --> 00:01:02,240
Understanding opportunity cost helps individuals as well as businesses make informed decisions that maximize their benefits and minimize their losses.

17
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:07,020
It's important to note that opportunity cost is not always measured in financial terms.

18
00:01:07,020 --> 00:01:12,050
It can also be related to factors like time, convenience or personal satisfaction.

19
00:01:12,050 --> 00:01:15,668
For that reason, opportunity cost can be difficult to quantify,

20
00:01:15,668 --> 00:01:18,200
especially when dealing with non monetary factors.

21
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:22,100
Have you ever had to make a decision that included an opportunity cost?

22
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:26,080
Take a few minutes now to think and then I'll ask for volunteers to share.
